course: everyday
lesson: 461
title: How best friends start
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Maddie and Lily became best friends on the first day of Reception, because they both cried and the teacher sat them on the same squashy beanbag. «Besties since the beanbag,» Maddie says. Five years later they still call it «beanbag day» and share a biscuit on its anniversary. Lily asks if they'll still be friends when they're old. «Obvs,» says Maddie. «We'll be the two old ladies at bingo.» Big friendships often start with something tiny.
explain: Sam and Jake became mates when Jake laughed at Sam's joke about the dinner lady's gravy («it's not gravy, it's a brown lake»). Nobody else got it. «Bruv, that's jokes,» Jake snorted — London slang where «bruv» means mate and «jokes» means hilarious. Connor met Raj at football: both were rubbish, both got stuck in defence, and they spent the whole match chatting about Minecraft. They stayed on the team two years, still terrible, still laughing.
explain: Chloe tried to buy friends. She bought sweets for the popular girls every day for a week. When the money ran out, so did they. She ended up sitting with Aisha, who shared her crisps for nothing, and they became proper friends. Mia's new classmate Zoe is from Texas. On day two she says, «Y'all wanna come over Saturday?» — «y'all» is Southern US for «you all». Mia didn't think anyone would ask her that fast. One brave invite started it all.
explain: Best friends don't have to do everything together. Sam wants chess club; Jake says chess is «mid» — Gen Z for boring, nothing special. They argue, then Sam goes to chess and Jake to football, and they still sit together at lunch. When Maddie gives Lily half a «best friend» necklace, Chloe's face drops; so the three of them make friendship bracelets instead. When Kieran and his best mate Tom get split into different Year 4 classes, Tom texts: «lowkey gutted ngl» — quietly very upset, not gonna lie. And some best mates are family: Sam and his cousin Ollie build a top-secret den in Grandad's garden every holiday, and Grandad pretends not to know who built it.
fact: Friendships often start with something small: a shared joke, a shared fear, sitting next to each other.
fact: Finding the same daft things funny is a common way friendships begin.
fact: You don't have to be good at something to make friends doing it; showing up and being yourself matters more.
fact: You can't buy friends: if the friendship stops when the gifts stop, it wasn't friendship.
fact: Inviting someone first is a kind, brave act, and many friendships start because one person asks.
fact: Best friends can like different things and do different hobbies and still be close.
fact: Showing a best friend you care should not make others feel shut out.
fact: When friends are split up, small efforts like break-time chats help keep the friendship going.
fact: Some friendships last a lifetime, kept alive by lots of small calls and visits over years.
fact: Cousins, brothers and sisters can be best friends too.
fact: Judge a friend by how they treat you, not only by what others say about them.
fact: «Mid» is Gen Z slang for boring or average, «lowkey» means a bit or quietly, «ngl» means «not gonna lie», and «y'all» is Southern US for «you all».
exercise: How did Maddie and Lily become best friends? | Both cried on day one and shared a beanbag | They both won a race together on sports day | Their mums made them | They met at a birthday party
exercise: What started Sam and Jake's friendship? | Jake laughed at Sam's gravy joke | Sam lent Jake his new football at break | They both got detention | Jake saved Sam from a dog
exercise: What did Connor and Raj have in common at football? | Both were rubbish and loved Minecraft | Both were star strikers who scored every week | Both hated each other | Both were the coach's sons
exercise: What happened when Chloe's sweet money ran out? | The popular girls stopped being friendly | The girls started buying her sweets back instead | They became best friends forever | She got more money from Nan
exercise: What does Chloe's sweet story teach? | You can't buy real friends | Better sweets buy better friends | Popular girls are always kind | Sharing crisps is a mistake
exercise: «Y'all wanna come over?» What does «y'all» mean? | You all | Yesterday | Your auntie | Hello there
exercise: Why did Zoe's invite matter so much to Mia? | Someone asking first made her feel welcome | Mia had always wanted to see Zoe's horse up close | Zoe had the best games | Mia's mum told her to go
exercise: Jake says chess is «mid». What does he mean? | Boring, nothing special | In the middle of the table | Brilliant and really exciting | Too hard for him to play
exercise: Sam goes to chess, Jake to football. What happens to their friendship? | They still sit together at lunch | They never speak again | They both quit everything | Jake joins chess too, just to keep Sam
exercise: Why did Chloe look hurt about the necklace? | She felt left out of the friendship | She wanted to buy one to sell at school | She thought it was ugly | She'd lost her own necklace
exercise: How did the girls fix the necklace problem? | All three made friendship bracelets | Maddie threw the necklace away | Chloe got a bigger necklace | Lily gave Chloe her half
exercise: «Lowkey gutted ngl.» What is Tom saying? | He's quietly very upset, not gonna lie | He's very happy and loud about it, honestly | He's lost his key | He's going to bed early
exercise: How can Kieran and Tom keep their friendship after being split up? | Meet up at break and at weekends | Refuse to talk to new kids | Ask the school to move Tom | Stop being friends at once
exercise: What keeps a friendship lasting for years? | Lots of small calls and visits | One very big present | Never arguing about anything at all | Living in the same house
exercise: Sam's best mate is also his cousin Ollie. Can that be true? | Yes, family can be best friends too | No, cousins don't count as real friends | Only if they're the same age | Only if they live together
test: «bruv just get her loads of sweets, she'll be ur bestie». Will that work? | No, friends you buy go when the sweets go | Yes, sweets make friends for life | Yes, but only chocolate | No, she'd want crisps
test: Raj: «we're both trash at footy lol». Should they quit the team? | No, being rubbish together can be how friends are made | Yes, because only good players ever make friends on a team | Yes, they'll never get picked | No, they should cheat to win
test: «a best friend has to do everything with you, otherwise what's the point». Is that true? | No, best friends can have different hobbies | Yes, or else it's not a real best friend at all | Yes, at least on weekdays | No, best friends never meet
test: If Zoe had never asked Mia over, what might have happened? | They might never have become friends | Mia would have asked a horse | Zoe would have moved away without saying | Nothing would have changed
test: «ur being dead mid about chess, it's actually sick». What does the speaker think? | Chess isn't boring, it's great | Chess is ill | Chess is too hard | Chess is in the middle of the park
test: In a film, two kids become best friends after one big adventure. What's more usual in real life? | Lots of small shared moments | Saving each other from dragons | One huge birthday party together | A teacher making them friends
test: Maddie wants to give Lily a necklace in front of the whole class. Chloe is right there. Kindest choice? | Do it in a way that doesn't shut Chloe out | Show it off so Chloe knows where she stands now | Give it, then tell Chloe to go away | Ask Chloe to pay for one
test: Sam's mum isn't sure about Jake because of his fights. How should she judge him? | By how he treats Sam | Only by his reputation | By the size of his house | By what his trainers cost
test: «we've been best mates since nursery but she's in the other class now, it's over». Is it? | No, small efforts can keep it going | Yes, classes end friendships | Yes, after one week apart | No, she must change class
test: Lily's mum still phones her school best friend every Sunday, thirty years later. What does that show? | Some friendships last a lifetime | Mums have no new friends | Phones are better than visits | Only grown-ups have best friends
test: «nobody gets my jokes except him lol». Why could that start a friendship? | Sharing the same sense of humour bonds people | Jokes make people scared | It means he's only being polite to you to be nice | It only works for gravy jokes
exam: Identify the principal flaw in an attempt to secure friendship by the daily purchase of confectionery for others. | The resulting attachment ceases when the gifts cease | Confectionery is too costly for most pupils | Friendship can only be purchased with crisps | The recipients must reciprocate in equal measure
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the pursuit of differing hobbies by two close friends? | It is compatible with an enduring friendship | It necessarily terminates the friendship at once | It is permissible only on weekends | It requires the consent of both parents
exam: Given that two close friends are assigned to separate classes, ascertain the course most likely to preserve their friendship. | Continued contact at break times and weekends | Refusal to associate with any new classmates | A demand that the school reverse its decision | The immediate termination of the friendship
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the origin of many friendships. | They frequently arise from small shared moments | They arise solely from shared adventures of great danger | They are invariably arranged by teachers | They arise only between pupils of equal ability
